tense relations between the u.s and  china have dominated a security summit  in singapore over the weekend though the  two sides did sit down for talks chinese  defense minister general wei fang hay  accused his u.s counterpart of hijacking  the support of asia-pacific countries to  turn them against beijing earlier u.s  defense secretary lloyd austin said  china or the prc was causing instability  with its claim to taiwan and increasing  military activity in the region  also stand by our friends as they uphold  their rights  and that's especially important  as a prc adopts a more coercive  and aggressive approach  to its territorial claims  for his part the chinese defense  minister said beijing would be prepared  to invade taiwan in certain  circumstances  if anyone dares to split taiwan from  china  we the chinese army  will not hesitate to go to war  no matter the cost and we will fight to  the end  let's speak now to dr lynn quark a  senior fellow for asia pacific security  at the international institute for  strategic studies she joins me from  singapore welcome to dw news asia  we heard some rather testy comments  there from the chinese and u.s defense  chiefs what's your sense of where this  meeting left ties between the two  countries  thank you for having me jared um i think  first the first point to note is that  this is the first meeting that um  military leaders from the united states  and china  have have had with each other since uh  president biden took office so i think  that's a very positive first step i mean  both sides um exchange testy words but  um i think the fact that there weren't  um fireworks um  was uh encouraging that said um this  must be the first step of many uh steps  necessary to um uh help ameliorate  tensions between the united states and  china and i think broadly speaking what  is more concerning for me is that we're  hearing less and less  from the united states or from china  about their desire to search for common  ground  let's move on and speak about taiwan  because this was also a focus point  we've also heard china made some quite  forceful comments on this issue as did  the us do you think a chinese invasion  of taiwan is more or less likely now  china's calculations will whether to  invade taiwan  would be based primarily on whether or  not it saw itself as able to win  a war  that involved the united states quickly  or at all  the fact that russia has invaded ukraine  is not going to change its calculations  on that matter and i think china's  calculations as i understand it is that  you know it's not it will not be able to  win a war involving the united states  for at least another 10 years so um i  think if anything the war uh  uh in ukraine and how it's panned out  there might make uh china even more  reluctant uh to uh take military action  against uh taiwan in the near to medium  term  i'd like to ask you about the south  china sea which i understand is one of  your areas of expertise and beijing's  disputed claims  china has called for quote turning the  south china sea into a sea of peace  friendship and cooperation do you think  this signifies any kind of a different  approach from beijing  right now what the problem is isn't what  china says it's what china does and what  we've seen in the south china sea is  continued militarization of land  features in the south china sea um we've  seen china continuing its encroachments  into the exclusive economic zones of  coastal states  we've also seen china send  hundreds at times of vessels to surround  features that are occupied  or administered by other countries in  the region now just briefly if you could  relations between australia and china  haven't been great in recent years to  say the least there's a new government  in canberra now  could this be a turning point  um the deputy prime minister of  australia and defense minister of  australia  richard miles spoke at the shangri-la  dialogue i think his words were very  well chosen they were  of a moderate tone um but at the end of  the day australia is going to be taking  a principled approach and it's not going  to be allowing china to exercise um  economic coercion against it as we've  seen china exert in the past and so i'm  not sure that we are likely to see  improvements or relations between  australia and china if china continues  with  actions that are problematic to say the  least uh vis-a-vis australia now if  china were to improve its behaviors  so that it accorded um with uh  principles of international law um so  that it stopped uh  economic coercion against australia  perhaps then we might see  relations improve but i think the ball  in that respect is in china's court  that was dr lin kwok from the  international institute for strategic  studies we really appreciate your  analysis thank you very much
 

